# X++ Script to Create a Custom REST Service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations

Below is a complete example of how to create a custom REST service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations (D365 F&O) using X++.

## 1. Create a Data Contract Class

First, create a data contract class to define the structure of your request/response data:

```xpp
[DataContractAttribute]
class MyCustomRestServiceDataContract
{
    str customerId;
    str customerName;
    str email;
    real creditLimit;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the CustomerId
/// </summary>
/// <returns>CustomerId</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('CustomerId')]
public str parmCustomerId(str _customerId = customerId)
{
    customerId = _customerId;
    return customerId;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the CustomerName
/// </summary>
/// <returns>CustomerName</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('CustomerName')]
public str parmCustomerName(str _customerName = customerName)
{
    customerName = _customerName;
    return customerName;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the Email
/// </summary>
/// <returns>Email</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('Email')]
public str parmEmail(str _email = email)
{
    email = _email;
    return email;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the CreditLimit
/// </summary>
/// <returns>CreditLimit</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('CreditLimit')]
public real parmCreditLimit(real _creditLimit = creditLimit)
{
    creditLimit = _creditLimit;
    return creditLimit;
}
```

## 2. Create a Service Class

Next, create the service class that will contain your business logic:

```xpp
class MyCustomRestService
{
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets customer information by customer account
/// </summary>
/// <param name="_customerId">Customer account</param>
/// <returns>Customer data contract</returns>
public MyCustomRestServiceDataContract getCustomerInfo(str _customerId)
{
    MyCustomRestServiceDataContract dataContract = new MyCustomRestServiceDataContract();
    CustTable custTable;

    select firstOnly custTable
        where custTable.AccountNum == _customerId;

    if (custTable)
    {
        dataContract.parmCustomerId(custTable.AccountNum);
        dataContract.parmCustomerName(custTable.name());
        dataContract.parmEmail(custTable.email());
        dataContract.parmCreditLimit(custTable.CreditMax);
    }

    return dataContract;
}

/// <summary>
/// Updates customer information
/// </summary>
/// <param name="_dataContract">Customer data contract</param>
/// <returns>Success flag</returns>
public boolean updateCustomerInfo(MyCustomRestServiceDataContract _dataContract)
{
    boolean ret = false;
    CustTable custTable;

    ttsBegin;
    select forUpdate custTable
        where custTable.AccountNum == _dataContract.parmCustomerId();

    if (custTable)
    {
        custTable.Name = _dataContract.parmCustomerName();
        custTable.email = _dataContract.parmEmail();
        custTable.CreditMax = _dataContract.parmCreditLimit();
        custTable.update();

        ret = true;
    }
    ttsCommit;

    return ret;
}
```

## 3. Create a Service Contract Class

Now create the service contract that defines the service operations:

```xpp
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
class MyCustomRestServiceContract
{
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets customer information
/// </summary>
/// <param name="_customerId">Customer account</param>
/// <returns>Customer data contract</returns>
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true),
 AifCollectionTypeAttribute('return', Types::Class, classStr(MyCustomRestServiceDataContract))]
public MyCustomRestServiceDataContract getCustomerInfo(str _customerId)
{
    MyCustomRestService service = new MyCustomRestService();
    return service.getCustomerInfo(_customerId);
}

/// <summary>
/// Updates customer information
/// </summary>
/// <param name="_dataContract">Customer data contract</param>
/// <returns>Success flag</returns>
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
public boolean updateCustomerInfo(MyCustomRestServiceDataContract _dataContract)
{
    MyCustomRestService service = new MyCustomRestService();
    return service.updateCustomerInfo(_dataContract);
}
```

## 4. Create a Service Group

Create a service group to expose your service:

1. In the AOT, right-click on **Service Groups** and select **New Service Group**
2. Name it "MyCustomRestServiceGroup"
3. Add your service to the group:
   - Right-click on the service group and select **New Service Node Reference**
   - Set the **Service** property to "MyCustomRestServiceContract"

## 5. Deploy the Service

To deploy the service:

1. Right-click on the service group and select **Deploy Service Group**
2. After deployment, you can find the service endpoint in:
   - **System administration > Setup > Services and Application Integration Framework > Inbound ports**

## 6. Test the Service

You can test your REST service using tools like Postman or cURL:

### GET Request Example:
```
GET https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestServiceContract/getCustomerInfo?customerId=US-001
Headers:
- Authorization: Bearer {your-access-token}
- Content-Type: application/json
```

### POST Request Example:
```
POST https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestServiceContract/updateCustomerInfo
Headers:
- Authorization: Bearer {your-access-token}
- Content-Type: application/json

Body:
{
    "CustomerId": "US-001",
    "CustomerName": "Updated Customer Name",
    "Email": "updated@customer.com",
    "CreditLimit": 10000
}
```

## Additional Notes:

1. **Authentication**: You'll need to authenticate using OAuth 2.0. Register an application in Azure AD and use the client credentials flow.

2. **Error Handling**: Consider adding proper error handling in your service methods.

3. **Logging**: Add logging to track service usage and errors.

4. **Security**: Ensure proper security roles are assigned to users who need to access this service.

5. **Documentation**: Document your service endpoints and data contracts for API consumers.

